dict(img=None,
stem="A 60-year-old woman with stable angina takes sublingual nitroglycerin. Within minutes her blood pressure falls from 138/84 to 112/70 mm Hg and her heart rate rises from 74 to 98/min. Her physician plans maintenance therapy with a second drug to blunt this effect.{FIG}Which drug class best complements the nitrate in this way?",
choices=["β-blocker","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","α1-adrenergic agonist","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor","Loop diuretic"], key=0,
exp="Vasodilation → BP falls → stretch receptors sense it → sympathetic outflow → <b>reflex tachycardia</b>, which raises O₂ demand. That's why nitrates are <b>paired with a β-blocker</b>: the reflex runs through sympathetic β1 outflow. Board add-on: each drug cancels the other's unwanted effect. The β-blocker stops the reflex tachycardia, and the nitrate offsets the β-blocker's rise in end-diastolic volume. Both lower O₂ demand.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Nitrates cause baroreflex tachycardia, so they are paired with a β-blocker, which blocks the reflex while the nitrate offsets the β-blocker's rise in preload.",
wrong={1:"A dihydropyridine also vasodilates and can <b>add</b> reflex tachycardia.",
2:"An α1 agonist would raise afterload and O₂ demand.",
3:"A PDE5 inhibitor with a nitrate causes refractory hypotension.",
4:"A diuretic doesn't block the reflex tachycardia."},
eli5="When nitroglycerin drops blood pressure, the body speeds the heart up to compensate, which wastes oxygen. A β-blocker stops that speed-up."),

